#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559} :activity/id #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180757 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" :step/math-academy-id 44660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179431 :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378504}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" :step/math-academy-id 44661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180758 :tutorial/content "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7698 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" :step/math-academy-id 44664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179432 :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378518}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" :step/math-academy-id 44665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179433 :question/id #uuid "c5e70edd-5a39-5e85-9461-68df182780dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" :step/math-academy-id 44668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" :step/math-academy-id 44704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180761 :tutorial/content "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7706 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" :step/math-academy-id 44705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180762 :tutorial/content "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7710 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" :step/math-academy-id 44723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179860}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970} :activity/id #uuid "13211b98-9cdf-5456-a254-0f2d03a88cbb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c52d4e-2631-53e7-8a00-72ab68871a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257401} {:answer/id #uuid "746b1cdb-7c35-5efe-a92c-2cd06400438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} {:answer/id #uuid "a34beb49-2bb7-5107-84ce-cae801ce1c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258211} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2b02b6-f203-59a0-ab8f-6e907c4aac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258857} {:answer/id #uuid "f75572fb-3d5c-57c1-b45a-ea11a4c996bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e69744-50ea-5409-9359-8197e1464b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08a12b2e-6f73-5fc0-987c-f4d58e5bc21f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124241" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{5}{4}+(-\\frac{9}{4})+2=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d9d1d09-adca-5f60-87c8-6eda1712954f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "57752ad6-299b-53a6-80c6-38f70d7331f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "8adcf20b-9143-567f-8916-5a2f8686af25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f998697-e815-585e-b0b5-3820b9ab9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406958} {:answer/id #uuid "51054506-a98c-59d1-82db-eab8461f2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406959} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4eee2-e47a-55c2-b5f0-e42eb70ff849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406962} {:answer/id #uuid "c3375fbb-f890-5cba-a4b4-7f53a73a78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} :answer-field/id #uuid "20065c9e-4427-527a-a708-a46b3d2f2065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124289" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v-1,\\,y=3v-2u-6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax+y+9,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-1 \\\\ 3v-2u-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -2u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+1 \\\\ y+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6) \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3x+y+9 \\\\ 2x+y+8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=3x+y+9,\\,v=2x+y+8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+2+8=13.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7332e33-ee2f-5908-9f85-c258b5d80b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c564b67d-ab6c-5a8c-9962-63cefc9e226d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406955}]}
